    <p>American Express (AXP) has delivered one of the most remarkable long-term compounding stories in U.S. equity markets. From an adjusted closing price of <strong>${kpi['start_price']:.2f}</strong> in June 1972 to <strong>${kpi['end_price']:.2f}</strong> as of March 2026, AXP generated a cumulative total return of <strong>{fmt_pct(kpi['cumulative_return_pct'])}</strong> over 53.8 years. This translates to a compounded annual growth rate (CAGR) of <strong>{kpi['cagr_pct']}%</strong> — a performance that would have turned a $10,000 investment in 1972 into approximately <strong>${10_000 * (1 + kpi['cumulative_return_pct']/100):,.0f}</strong> today, before dividends.</p>

    <p>The stock reached its all-time high of <strong>${meta['at_all_time_high']:.2f}</strong> on {meta['ath_date']}, reflecting strong post-COVID recovery momentum. As of the most recent trading date ({meta['end_date']}), AXP trades approximately <strong>{(meta['recent_price']/meta['at_all_time_high']*100):.0f}%</strong> below that peak. The all-time adjusted low was <strong>${meta['at_all_time_low']:.2f}</strong> in {meta['atl_date']}, underscoring the enormous wealth creation across five decades.</p>

    <p>AXP's journey reflects the broader evolution of the financial services industry — surviving inflation crises of the 1970s, thriving during the 1980s–1990s credit expansion, enduring the 2008 Global Financial Crisis (which hit financial stocks disproportionately), and rebounding through the post-COVID era. The stock's <strong>{kpi['annualized_volatility_pct']:.1f}%</strong> annualized volatility and <strong>{kpi['max_drawdown_pct']:.1f}%</strong> maximum drawdown highlight the material risk borne by long-term shareholders, particularly during financial sector distress.</p>
