      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 2: LONG-TERM PRICE TREND ANALYSIS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="price-trend-analysis">
  <h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
  <div class="section-obj">Analysis of the closing price trend across decades, identifying major bull and bear phases, all-time highs and lows, and CAGR over the full period and key sub-periods.</div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartPrice"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>The long-term price chart reveals AXP's transformation from a niche charge-card company into a global financial services powerhouse. The adjusted price remained below $10 through the 1970s and 1980s, reflecting both the company's smaller scale and the challenging macroeconomic environment of high inflation and interest rates. The stock gained meaningful traction in the 1990s as AXP expanded its card network and merchant services, surging into the dot-com era before being cut down in the subsequent bear market.</p>

    <p>The 2000s were the "lost decade" — AXP peaked in mid-2007 near $70 before the Global Financial Crisis collapsed it to $11.30 by March 2009, an 83.9% drawdown that exceeded even the broader financial sector's decline. The recovery since 2009 has defined the modern AXP narrative: a steady climb through the 2010s, a sharp but brief COVID-19 decline in early 2020, and an accelerating post-COVID rally that took the stock above $380 in late 2025.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Sub-Period Performance</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
