<!-- SECTION 3: VOLATILITY & RISK ASSESSMENT -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="volatility-risk">
  <h2>Volatility & Risk Assessment</h2>
  <div class="section-obj">Examination of daily and annualized volatility, maximum drawdowns, and periods of extreme price movement, providing a comprehensive risk profile of the stock.</div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartDrawdown"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>The drawdown chart above reveals AXP's risk fingerprint. Drawdowns exceeding 20% are common and expected — the stock has experienced at least <strong>{len([p for p in a['phases'] if p['type'] == 'bear'])}</strong> such episodes since 1972. What distinguishes AXP's risk profile is the severity of its worst drawdowns during financial sector crises, where the stock can decline 60–84% from peak to trough.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Key Risk Observation:</strong> AXP's three worst drawdowns all occurred during systemic financial stress — the 1973–74 bear market (oil crisis), the 2008–09 Global Financial Crisis (83.9%), and the 2020 COVID-19 crash. The stock's beta-like behavior amplifies macroeconomic shocks because AXP's business model — credit cards, lending, and transaction processing — is highly correlated with consumer spending and credit conditions. During the 2008 crisis specifically, AXP fell harder than the broader market (S&P 500 declined ~57% peak-to-trough vs. AXP's 83.9%), reflecting the market's acute concern about credit losses and funding liquidity for financial institutions.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartRollingVol"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>The rolling 1-year annualized volatility tells a story of changing risk regimes. Volatility spiked dramatically during the 2008 financial crisis (approaching 100%), the COVID-19 crash (above 80%), and during several other market dislocations. The long-term average annualized volatility of <strong>{kpi['annualized_volatility_pct']:.1f}%</strong> is elevated relative to the broader market (~16% for the S&P 500), consistent with AXP's profile as a financial sector stock with inherent leverage sensitivity.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartReturnDist"></canvas>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 4: TRADING VOLUME ANALYSIS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="volume-analysis">
  <h2>Trading Volume Analysis</h2>
  <div class="section-obj">Analysis of trading volume trends over time, structural shifts in liquidity, and correlation of volume spikes with major price events.</div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartVolume"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>Trading volume data reveals several structural shifts in AXP's market liquidity. Volume was effectively zero in the earliest records (1972-era), as comprehensive volume data was not available for that period. From the 1980s onward, daily trading volume grew steadily, reflecting the broader expansion of equity market participation and the rise of institutional trading.</p>

    <p>Notable volume spikes coincide with major price events: the 1987 Black Monday crash, the 2008–09 financial crisis (where 20M+ share days became common), the 2020 COVID-19 sell-off, and various earnings events. The 2008–09 period stands out with sustained elevated volume — investors aggressively repositioned in AXP as fears about its financial stability peaked.</p>

    <p>In recent years, daily volume has generally ranged between 2–6 million shares, with spikes above 10 million during earnings releases and significant market-wide events. This represents mature, liquid trading conditions for a large-cap financial stock.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Volume by Decade</h3>
