    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Split Ratio</th><th>Price at Split ($)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{''.join(f'<tr><td>{s["date"]}</td><td>{s["ratio"]:.0f}:1</td><td>${s["close"]:.2f}</td></tr>' for s in a['split_events'])}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>''' if a['split_events'] else ''}
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 6: DECADE-BY-DECADE PERFORMANCE COMPARISON -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="decade-comparison">
  <h2>Decade-by-Decade Performance Comparison</h2>
  <div class="section-obj">Comparison of AXP's performance across each decade in terms of returns, volatility, and dividend activity, contextualized within broader economic environments.</div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <canvas id="chartDecadeReturns"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="chartDecadeVol"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <canvas id="chartDecadeCagr"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Start $</th><th>End $</th><th>Total Return</th><th>CAGR</th><th>Max Drawdown</th><th>Ann. Volatility</th><th>Dividends</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>{decade_rows}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>The decade-by-decade breakdown reveals starkly different investing environments:</p>
    <p><strong>1970s:</strong> A period of modest nominal gains against the backdrop of stagflation, oil shocks, and high interest rates. AXP's adjusted price grew from ~$1.22 to ~$1.30 — essentially flat as inflation ravaged purchasing power.</p>
    <p><strong>1980s:</strong> The Reagan-era bull market and financial deregulation propelled AXP substantially higher. The 1987 Black Monday crash appears as a sharp but brief dip in the data. Returns were strong as credit card adoption accelerated.</p>
    <p><strong>1990s:</strong> The strongest decade for AXP shareholders — the combination of the tech boom, expanding card networks, and the rise of consumer credit drove the stock to dramatic gains. The decade saw exceptional CAGR despite the 1998 Asian/Russian crisis turbulence.</p>
    <p><strong>2000s:</strong> The "lost decade" for equities generally, and catastrophic for financial stocks specifically. AXP suffered two massive drawdowns — the dot-com bust (2000–2002) and the Global Financial Crisis (2007–2009), which produced the stock's worst-ever peak-to-trough decline of 83.9%. Decade returns were negative.</p>
    <p><strong>2010s:</strong> A steady, grinding recovery as AXP rebuilt from the financial crisis. Strong positive returns with moderate volatility, supported by the longest economic expansion in U.S. history, rising consumer spending, and aggressive share buybacks.</p>
    <p><strong>2020s (partial):</strong> The COVID-19 shock produced a rapid drawdown and an equally rapid recovery. The subsequent period delivered exceptional returns as AXP benefited from resilient consumer spending, travel recovery, and higher interest rates boosting net interest income.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- CLOSING NOTES -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="closing">
  <h2>Conclusion</h2>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>American Express (AXP) has been — and remains — a storied wealth compounder. A shareholder who bought at the 1972 adjusted price and held through all market cycles would have multiplied their capital by nearly <strong>250x</strong> (price-only) over five decades. This performance substantiates the buy-and-hold thesis for high-quality financial franchises with durable competitive advantages.</p>

    <p>However, the analysis also surfaces three critical risk considerations for prospective and current investors:</p>
    <ol style="margin-left:20px;">
      <li><strong>Financial sector concentration risk:</strong> AXP's 83.9% drawdown during 2007–2009 is a permanent reminder that financial stocks can suffer catastrophic losses during systemic credit events — declines far in excess of the broader market.</li>
      <li><strong>Volatility tolerance:</strong> With ~35% annualized volatility, AXP requires an investor to withstand frequent 20%+ drawdowns. The stock has historically declined 20%+ in more than a dozen separate episodes.</li>
      <li><strong>Dividend income is supplementary, not primary:</strong> Total dividends of ${kpi['total_dividends_per_share']:.2f} over 50+ years are welcome but pale beside the capital appreciation component. Investors seeking current income should supplement AXP with higher-yielding names.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>For investors with adequate risk tolerance and a multi-year time horizon, AXP's historical record supports its role as a core portfolio holding — but position sizing should account for the possibility of deep, prolonged drawdowns during the next financial cycle.</p>
